| A declaration may carry a kind. Runtime values stay dynamic, but `code check`
| validates the annotation whenever the assigned value is statically knowable.
| The annotation is still optional, so it is only a contract where written.
port ∈ Number = 8080
assert port = 8080
assert port ∈ Number

| Wrong at runtime, because annotations do not coerce values. `code check`
| reports this mismatch.
mislabelled ∈ Number = "8080"
assert mislabelled ∈ String

| Object fields take one too, and mean exactly what they mean without it.
request = { url ∈ String = "https://example.com", retries ∈ Number = 3 }
assert request = { url = "https://example.com", retries = 3 }

| A computed key can be annotated as well — the annotation sits between the
| name and the `=`, wherever the name came from.
field = "Accept"
headers = { "$field" ∈ String = "text/plain" }
assert headers["Accept"] = "text/plain"

| And a handler's field list.
Greet { who ∈ String } =>
    return Greeting { text ∈ String = "hello, $who" }

emit Greet { who = "ada" } to this get r
assert r.text = "hello, ada"
assert r ∈ Greeting

| A class name is a name too, so an annotation may say what a binding is
| expected to be tagged.
reply ∈ Greeting = r
assert reply ∈ Greeting
